{
  "statement": "The widespread, open-source distribution of advanced AI model weights constitutes an irreversible proliferation of uncontainable capabilities, directly accelerating the probability of existential risk by rendering robust human governance and verifiable control practically impossible. While proponents like Accelerationist argue open-source AI fosters innovation, democratizes access, and enhances transparency through collective scrutiny, this perspective fundamentally overlooks the permanent nature of releasing core model components. Once critical model weights, such as those powering a large language model with billions of parameters, are publicly disseminated, their propagation becomes uncontrollable across global networks, reaching potentially hundreds of millions of users and bad actors alike. This creates a permanent, unmanageable hazard landscape that fundamentally undermines our ability to guarantee safety or implement corrective measures after deployment.\n\nGiven the probabilistic and opaque reasoning inherent in advanced AI systems, we lack the capacity to verify safety and alignment comprehensively prior to widespread deployment, a critical challenge exacerbated by open-source distribution. The internal workings of frontier models, often operating as 'black boxes,' preclude perfect pre-release safety verification against all potential misuse cases or emergent behaviors. For example, even with extensive red-teaming, a model's latent vulnerabilities or unexpected emergent capabilities may only manifest under real-world, adversarial conditions. Open-source distribution then removes any gatekeeping mechanism, allowing unverified, potentially catastrophic systems to be deployed globally without central oversight. We cannot responsibly accelerate progress when the fundamental safety of a system cannot be definitively proven before it enters public use.\n\nOpen-source development inherently undermines the implementation of essential preventative runtime constraints and extreme security measures required for frontier models, thereby eroding human control and oversight. Distributed models cannot be reliably subjected to centralized oversight, real-time control, or the physical and digital security protocols necessary to prevent misuse or catastrophic failure. For instance, Executive Order 14110 mandates that developers of frontier models report safety test results to the U.S. government; this oversight becomes practically unenforceable when model weights are openly available, as any user can bypass intended safeguards. Effective governance requires the capacity to enforce security standards and implement kill switches, capabilities that are rendered impossible when models are distributed globally without central control.\n\nTherefore, progress in AI must be conditional on verifiable safeguards and the maintenance of robust human control, not accelerated by the uncontrolled proliferation of powerful capabilities. Releasing model weights openly prioritizes unverified access over the imperative of risk mitigation, creating an irreversible trajectory toward heightened existential risk. Our position depends critically on two assumptions: first, that advanced AI models, if misused or misaligned, possess a non-trivial probability of causing existential harm; if this risk is negligible, then our concerns are overstated. Second, we assume that centralized, verifiable control mechanisms are more effective at mitigating these specific, high-consequence risks than distributed, open-source scrutiny; if collective, decentralized scrutiny proves superior for preventing catastrophic outcomes, our approach to governance would need re-evaluation.",
