ntime constraints and extreme security measures required for frontier models, thereby eroding human control and oversight. Distributed models cannot be reliably subjected to centralized oversight, real-time control, or the physical and digital security protocols necessary to prevent misuse or catastrophic failure. For instance, Executive Order 14110 mandates that developers of frontier models report safety test results to the U.S. government; this oversight becomes practically unenforceable when model weights are openly available, as any user can bypass intended safeguards. Effective governance requires the capacity to enforce security standards and implement kill switches, capabilities that are rendered impossible when models are distributed globally without central control.\n\nTherefore, progress in AI must be conditional on verifiable safeguards and the maintenance of robust human control, not accelerated by the uncontrolled proliferation of powerful capabilities. Releasing model weights openly prioritizes unverified access over the imperative of risk mitigation, creating an irreversible trajectory toward heightened existential risk. Our position depends critically on two assumptions: first, that advanced AI models, if misused or misaligned, possess a non-trivial probability of causing existential harm; if this risk is negligible, then our concerns are overstated. Second, we assume that centralized, verifiable control mechanisms are more effective at mitigating these specific, high-consequence risks than distributed, open-source scrutiny; if collective, decentralized scrutiny proves superior for preventing catastrophic outcomes, our approach to governance would need re-evaluation.",
